<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Chronic combat-related post-traumatic stress symptoms in a 40-year-old veteran, with chronic insomnia and depressive symptoms. Please confirm the diagnosis, provide trauma-focused psychotherapy, and advise on sleep, the depressive symptoms, and suicide risk. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks. No current suicidal ideation, intent, or plan; a collaborative safety plan was completed today. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does he meet criteria for post-traumatic stress disorder, and are the depressive symptoms part of it or a separate depressive disorder?</item><item>Is he a good candidate for trauma-focused psychotherapy such as Cognitive Processing Therapy, and can your service provide it?</item><item>How should the insomnia and nightmares be treated before any sleep medication is considered?</item><item>What is your formulation of his suicide risk, and do you have any changes to the safety plan?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Murphy was referred by Dr. Marquez at his intake visit six weeks ago. I evaluated him at the start of care and have since delivered five weekly sessions of Cognitive Processing Therapy; this report answers the referral questions. He describes intrusive memories and nightmares of combat, avoidance of reminders and crowds, beliefs about blame and constant danger, and hypervigilance with a strong startle, present for many years and made worse by poor sleep. He engages well and completes the practice assignments. Since starting therapy his nightmares have fallen from most nights to two or three a week, he falls asleep sooner with the agreed sleep routine, and he concentrates better at work. He has had no thoughts of suicide, intent, plan, or preparatory behavior since intake and keeps the safety plan with him. He uses no alcohol, drugs, or medicines. He has had no manic or hypomanic episodes and no psychotic symptoms. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Audie Leon Murphy was born June 20, 1924. [D]</item><item>Murphy served in World War II and became one of the most decorated United States soldiers of the war. [D]</item><item>Murphy was wounded in the leg during the January 26, 1945 action near Holtzwihr and was wounded three times during the war. [D]</item><item>Murphy experienced postwar nightmares and insomnia. [D]</item><item>Murphy experienced postwar depression and headaches. [D]</item><item>Murphy&apos;s postwar symptoms are retrospectively described as post-traumatic stress disorder. [R]</item><item>Murphy reported sleeping with a loaded pistol under his pillow. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

