    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Post-traumatic stress disorder, chronic, combat-related (F43.10). Confirmed on clinical interview against DSM-5-TR criteria, with intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in thinking and mood, and arousal symptoms all present at evaluation. He is responding to Cognitive Processing Therapy: the PCL-5 fell 11 points, from 42 to 31, a clinically meaningful change, but 31 is still near the probable-PTSD range, so the full course should be completed. [S]</item><item>Depressive symptoms. The low mood, poor sleep, and poor concentration track the PTSD symptoms and are improving with them (PHQ-9 11 to 7). There is no separate major depressive episode on interview and no history of mania or hypomania, so no separate depressive disorder is diagnosed and a bipolar screen is not needed. [S]</item><item>Chronic insomnia with trauma-related nightmares (G47.00). Improving with the sleep routine, used on five to six nights a week, and with the trauma-focused work. He reports no snoring, witnessed apneas, or daytime sleepiness. [S]</item><item>Suicide risk formulation. Acute risk is low: no current ideation, intent, plan, or preparatory behavior; active engagement in treatment; and strong protective factors in his family and peer supports, purposeful work, and service identity. Chronic risk is higher than in the general population because of combat exposure, PTSD, and chronic insomnia. The collaborative safety and lethal-means plan from intake is appropriate and needs no change. Outpatient care is appropriate. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Marquez&apos;s intake note and safety plan, the intake PCL-5 and PHQ-9, today&apos;s repeat scores, and his therapy practice assignments. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Complete the Cognitive Processing Therapy course, with a goal of at least eight sessions by the twelve-week review. [S]</item><item>No PTSD medication at present, given his preference and his response; reconsider only if the response stalls. [S]</item><item>Continue the sleep routine and stimulus control; offer cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia if the insomnia persists after the course; no sleep medication now. [S]</item><item>Avoid benzodiazepines and over-the-counter sedating sleep aids. [S]</item><item>No separate depression treatment; repeat the PHQ-9 with the PCL-5 at the twelve-week review. [S]</item><item>Review the safety plan at each primary-care visit and therapy session, keeping the lethal-means steps and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline (press 1 for the Veterans Crisis Line) in place. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Marquez today; Mr. Murphy agrees with the plan and has a copy of this report. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Weekly Cognitive Processing Therapy with me to the end of the course, then booster sessions as needed. Primary care repeats the PCL-5 and PHQ-9 at the twelve-week review. I will see him sooner for any suicidal thoughts or a stall in progress. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
